09:49:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Baldness usually affects scalp hair follicles, not all hair follicles in the body. In common male-pattern baldness, scalp follicles in certain areas are genetically sensitive to the hormone DHT (dihydrotestosterone). Over time, DHT makes those follicles shrink, producing thinner, shorter hairs until little or no visible scalp hair remains. Beard follicles respond differently to the same hormones. Androgens such as testosterone and DHT generally stimulate facial-hair growth, making beard hairs thicker and darker. So someone can lose scalp hair because their scalp follicles are DHT-sensitive while still growing a full beard because their facial follicles are stimulated by DHT. This is why baldness and beard growth can even occur together—though both vary widely based on genetics, age, hormones, and health.
