09:49:27 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
09:49:27 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=238
09:49:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The **hair itself** is broadly the same kind of material: a protein called **keratin**, much like nails and skin’s outer layer. But the important part is the **follicle**—the tiny living organ in the skin that makes each hair. Follicles in different body areas are programmed differently and respond differently to hormones. For example: - **Scalp follicles** in people prone to male-pattern baldness can shrink when exposed to DHT, eventually making very fine or nearly invisible hairs. - **Beard follicles** often do the opposite: testosterone and DHT help them produce thicker, darker “terminal” beard hairs. - **Arm, leg, eyebrow, and chest follicles** each have their own hormone sensitivity and growth cycle too. Nails are a useful comparison: fingernails and toenails are both keratin, but they grow at different rates and have somewhat different environments and functions. Similarly, hair shafts are similar material, but the follicles producing them are not interchangeable.
