09:49:38 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
09:49:38 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=484
09:49:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: They can feel very similar because both are thick, pigmented **terminal hairs** made mostly of keratin. In a given person, beard and scalp hairs may have similar diameter, curl, and surface texture, so your fingers may not detect much difference. But “similar hair” does not mean “identical follicle behavior.” The key differences are mostly beneath the skin and in how the follicle responds to signals: - **Hormone receptors differ by location.** A scalp follicle near the temples may shrink in response to DHT, while a beard follicle responds to DHT by growing larger and making thicker hair. - **Growth cycles differ.** Scalp hair usually stays in its active growth phase for years, allowing it to get long. Beard hair’s active phase is usually shorter, which is why it generally reaches a shorter maximum length. - **Genetic programming differs.** Follicles carry location-specific instructions—similar to how skin on your palm and skin on your face are both skin but behave differently. So the *shaft* you touch may be nearly the same material and texture. Baldness is mainly about whether particular **scalp follicles remain productive**, not about beard hair being made from a fundamentally different substance.
