09:49:51 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=775
09:49:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your barber is right in the practical sense: beard hair and scalp hair are both keratin hair shafts, and they can be very similar in texture, thickness, and how they behave when cut or styled—especially for a particular person. The distinction is about what happens at the follicle level over time, not something a barber necessarily needs to separate when working with the hair itself. A scalp follicle and a beard follicle can produce hair that feels almost identical, yet have different hormone receptors and growth-cycle settings. So these statements can both be true: - **“Beard hair and head hair are basically the same.”** True in material and everyday grooming terms. - **“Baldness can affect scalp hair without stopping beard growth.”** True because follicles in different locations can respond differently to hormones and genetics.
